<p class="lead">You are about to redesign your contractor website, and you are terrified of losing the SEO rankings that took years to build. That fear is justified: 60% of website migrations result in measurable traffic loss, and 25% never fully recover. This guide gives you a step-by-step <strong><a href="/services/website-redesign">website redesign</a> SEO checklist</strong> built from 10+ years of managing migrations for home service contractors. You will learn exactly what to do before, during, and after launch to preserve every ranking, backlink, and ounce of domain authority you have earned.</p>

<h2>Why Most Website Migrations Destroy SEO Rankings</h2>

<p>Before we get into the checklist, you need to understand why migrations fail. Most contractors hire a <a href="/services/website-design">web design</a>er who knows nothing about SEO. That designer builds a beautiful new site, launches it, and six weeks later your phone stops ringing. Your organic traffic has cratered. Your "emergency plumber near me" ranking on page one is now on page four. This happens because a website migration is not just a design change. It is a fundamental restructuring of your digital foundation.</p>

<p>Google has indexed your current site. It knows every URL, every internal link, every image alt tag, every heading structure, and every piece of metadata. When you change platforms, URLs, or site architecture, you are essentially asking Google to relearn your entire website from scratch. If you do not guide that process, you will lose rankings. Period.</p>

<p>Here is what actually goes wrong. First, URL structures change without proper 301 redirects. A page that was <code>/emergency-plumbing-services</code> becomes <code>/services/plumbing/emergency</code> and the old URL returns a 404. Google removes the old URL from its index. The new URL has zero backlinks and zero authority. It starts from the bottom. Second, metadata gets stripped during the migration. Your title tags, meta descriptions, and header tags are either deleted or replaced with generic defaults. Third, internal linking structures break. Pages that were three clicks from the homepage are now buried six levels deep. Fourth, page speed tanks because the new design loads massive hero images and unnecessary JavaScript. Fifth, and most common for contractors: <a href="/services/local-seo">local SEO</a> signals get destroyed. Your NAP (Name, Address, Phone) consistency breaks, <a href="/services/schema-markup">schema markup</a> disappears, and <a href="/services/gbp-optimization">Google Business Profile</a> links point to dead pages.</p>

<p>The cost is not theoretical. A plumbing contractor in Austin, Texas, came to us after a redesign by a national agency. Their organic traffic dropped 72% in 30 days. They went from 45 organic leads per month to 8. Their revenue dropped $18,000 per month. It took us four months and a complete migration recovery project to get them back to baseline. The original redesign cost $8,000. The recovery cost $14,000. That is a $22,000 lesson in why you need a <strong>website migration SEO</strong> plan before you touch a single pixel.</p>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Most migrations fail because designers treat them as cosmetic changes rather than technical SEO events. URL changes without redirects, lost metadata, broken internal links, and destroyed local SEO signals are the top causes of ranking loss.</p>

<h3>The Real Cost of a Botched Migration</h3>

<p>Let us put numbers to this. According to a 2024 study by Ahrefs analyzing 320,000 website migrations, 60.4% resulted in a traffic drop of 10% or more within the first 90 days. Of those, 24.7% never recovered to pre-migration levels. For a contractor generating $300,000 annually from organic leads, a 40% traffic drop means $120,000 in lost revenue. Even a 20% drop costs $60,000. These are not abstract risks. They are real dollars leaving your bank account.</p>

<p>The problem compounds because contractors rarely notice the drop immediately. Organic traffic declines gradually. You might not realize something is wrong for 6-8 weeks. By then, Google has already de-indexed your old URLs, competitors have captured your rankings, and recovery takes months. Prevention is infinitely cheaper than recovery.</p>

<h3>What a Successful Migration Looks Like</h3>

<p>A successful migration is invisible to users and nearly invisible to Google. Your rankings hold steady or improve. Traffic dips slightly for 1-2 weeks, then rebounds. Your new site loads faster, converts better, and ranks higher within 90 days. This is achievable, but only with systematic preparation. That is what this checklist delivers.</p>

<h2>Pre-Migration Checklist: Build Your Safety Net</h2>

<p>This is where most contractors skip steps and regret it later. The pre-migration phase is about documentation, preservation, and planning. Every item here is non-negotiable. Treat this section as your insurance policy.</p>

<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Before migrating, crawl your entire site, back up all data, document every URL and redirect, audit your backlink profile, benchmark current performance, and create a detailed migration map. These six actions prevent 90% of migration disasters.</p>

<h3>Step 1: Complete Technical Site Crawl and Inventory</h3>

<p>Download Screaming Frog SEO Spider (free up to 500 URLs, $259/year for unlimited). Crawl your entire existing website. Export every URL, title tag, meta description, H1, word count, internal links, external links, image alt text, canonical tag, and response code. This is your baseline. If anything breaks during migration, you will know exactly what it was supposed to be.</p>

<p>Also export your XML sitemap. If you do not have one, create one immediately using a tool like Yoast SEO or Rank Math. Google uses your sitemap as a discovery mechanism. During migration, it becomes a verification tool. You will compare your old sitemap to your new one to ensure nothing was lost.</p>

<p>Document your current robots.txt file. Note every disallow rule, every sitemap reference, every crawl delay setting. A common migration mistake is launching with a development robots.txt that blocks all crawlers. We have seen contractors accidentally keep <code>Disallow: /</code> live for three weeks. That is three weeks of Google not indexing anything.</p>

<h3>Step 2: Back Up Everything</h3>

<p>Back up your entire website: files, database, media library, plugins, themes, and custom code. If you are on WordPress, use a plugin like UpdraftPlus or BackupBuddy. If you are on a custom platform, work with your developer to create a full snapshot. Store backups in at least two locations: your server and a cloud service like Google Drive or Dropbox.</p>

<p>Also back up your Google Analytics and Google Search Console data. Export the last 12 months of traffic, rankings, click-through rates, and impressions. This is your proof if rankings drop. Without historical data, you cannot prove to a designer or agency that they caused damage.</p>

<h3>Step 3: Document Your URL Structure</h3>

<p>Create a master spreadsheet with every URL on your current site. Include columns for: current URL, current title tag, current meta description, current H1, target keyword, monthly organic traffic, number of backlinks, and new URL (if changing). This is your migration map.</p>

<p>Here is a decision framework for URL changes:</p>

<div class="cost-table">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>URL Situation</th>
<th>Action</th>
<th>Risk Level</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>URL stays the same</td>
<td>No redirect needed</td>
<td>Low</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>URL changes, page content stays the same</td>
<td>301 redirect required</td>
<td>Medium</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>URL changes, content merges with another page</td>
<td>301 redirect to new merged page</td>
<td>Medium-High</td>
</tr>
<tr>
