<h2>Your Migration Action Plan: From Checklist to Results</h2>

<p>You now have a complete <strong>website redesign SEO checklist</strong> built from real-world experience with home service contractors. Here is your condensed action plan:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Weeks 1-2 (Pre-Migration):</strong> Crawl your site, back up everything, document all URLs, audit backlinks, benchmark performance, create your redirect map, and preserve local SEO signals.</li>
<li><strong>Week 3 (Staging):</strong> Build the new site on staging, implement redirects, preserve metadata, maintain internal links, re-implement schema, verify analytics, and optimize images.</li>
<li><strong>Week 4 (Launch):</strong> Launch during low-traffic hours, submit your new sitemap, request indexing for top pages, and monitor Search Console hourly for the first 24 hours.</li>
<li><strong>Weeks 5-8 (Post-Launch):</strong> Daily Search Console checks, weekly traffic comparisons, fix all technical errors, update local citations, and rebuild internal links.</li>
<li><strong>Months 2-3 (Optimization):</strong> Request backlink updates, monitor Core Web Vitals, refine content, and measure ROI against pre-migration benchmarks.</li>
</ol>

<p>A website migration is not a design project. It is an SEO project with a design component. Treat it with the seriousness it deserves, and you will <strong>redesign without losing rankings</strong>. Treat it casually, and you will join the 60% of migrations that damage organic traffic.</p>

<p>The contractors who succeed are the ones who plan obsessively, execute carefully, and monitor relentlessly. They understand that a new website is an investment, not an expense. And they know that preserving SEO equity is just as important as creating a beautiful design.</p>

